course: dictionary
lesson: 5986
title: Meagre: small and not enough
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Meagre means small in amount, and not enough for what is needed. «She had a meagre meal of bread and water.» The clue is bread and water: there was very little food. Read the words around meagre and you can often see that the amount is small.
explain: The Mole notices «the narrow, meagre dimensions» of a house that has been empty for a long time. The house is small and poor, and the words around it, dust and deserted, show that it is not cared for. Meagre fits both the size and the poor condition.
explain: Meagre can also mean thin or lean, but this is an old meaning. You will mostly meet it as small or not enough, as in «meagre wages». In the UK, Australia and New Zealand we spell it meagre; in the US it is meager.
fact: Meagre is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like a meagre meal or meagre wages.
fact: «Meagre» means small in amount, not enough.
fact: «Meagre» can also mean thin or lean, but this is an old meaning.
fact: Check your meaning by swapping in a word like small or scanty: a meagre meal is a small meal.
fact: Spelling: meagre in the UK, Australia and New Zealand; meager in the US. Both mean the same.
exercise: «She had a meagre meal of bread and water.» What does meagre mean here? | Very tasty | Very long | Hot and steaming | *Small in amount, not enough
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as meagre? | Plenty | Generous | *Scanty | Huge
exercise: The Mole sees «narrow, meagre dimensions» in an old house. What is the best swap for meagre? | *Small and poor | Warm and cosy | Shiny and new | Loud and busy
test: Mum says the family's «meagre wages» barely pay for food. What does meagre mean? | Early | Secret | Large and generous | *Small, not enough
test: In an old story, a man is called meagre because he is thin and lean. What does meagre mean in this story? | Very rich | Very tall | *Thin | Loud and cheerful
exam: Which sentence uses meagre correctly? | The cake was meagre, with plenty of cream and jam. | Our team had a meagre win, and we celebrated with a big party. | The park was meagre, full of huge trees and lakes. | *The workers were paid meagre wages for long hours.
